## 12. Where Gate A stands: unmet requirements, accepted limits, later tasks

**Unmet requirements of the contract (`docs/judge.md`): none that this work found.** Checked: every G and I relation has a
scenario and a mutation, and every clause is observed by a valid campaign (28 of 28, 28 of 28 mutations by their expected
rule, 14 of 14 relations; `check-judge-matrix.py --clauses`); `calibration.tsv` has a measured row for each of the 14
concurrent scenarios, each following from its counts and the formula (`calibrate.py check`); the self-tests pass (131, 95, 58,
47, 15, 10 checks); the calibration job ran on GitHub and its artifact was checked independently. The partial-delivery document
(`judge-partial-delivery.md`) called Gate A 'open' because of `G8.a`, `I3.b` and the calibration; the first two are observed and
the third is measured.

**Accepted limits (they bound what the numbers mean; none is a requirement the contract adds):**
- Independence between executions is **assumed**, as the contract assumes it. Every bound is conditional on it. The one check made
  (first 40 executions against the rest) can refute a position-free rate and proves nothing when it passes.
- Every `N` is the contract's floor of 300. It is a valid result. `S07/M8` (22 of 200 on macOS, 87 on Linux) shows an intermittent
  defect also lands there. A rarer defect than the measured ones is covered by no row, which is true of any finite campaign.
- `S10` keeps its warning: its first 40 macOS executions detect less in two of three campaigns (pooled 0.89 against 0.97,
  an unplanned observation, p = 0.0011); on Linux there is no such drop. No campaign was repeated to make it disappear.
- Two calibrations exist and are kept apart: `judge/calibration.tsv` is the Linux one (GitHub Actions run 37982617368, `abddabe`,
  2 CPUs), chosen because it is the environment of the manual job and its evidence is a downloadable artifact; the macOS one
  (`4a30ba4`, 16 CPUs) is `judge/calibration.macos.tsv`, same format, validated in CI. Both give `N = 300` in all 14 rows. That
  does not make their probabilities interchangeable: `q_low` belongs to the machine named in the `cpus` column, and a change of
  hardware or runner requires a new calibration (`docs/judge.md` section 5, item 5).
- Five report rows are `timeout` and not counted: `M13/S05`, `M40/S05`, `M40/S06`, `M2/S10` and `M58/S07`, all concurrent. By
  reading the end condition of each scenario in `Judge.scala`, the mutant removes the event that ends the run (the same class as
  `S08`, `S11` and `S14` before this work); only `M58/S07` was confirmed from a log, the other four are not diagnosed by a run.
  Each of their clauses has another valid detection.
- `S08` and `S18` rest on Pekko draining the system messages after each user message and converting a notification inside the
  watcher's system processing. `selftest_pekko_source.py` fails if the pinned source stops saying so (checked with an altered
  `Mailbox.scala`), and `witness_check.py` checks the order on the campaign logs: `S08` and `S18` baseline, 200 concurrent and 1
  controlled log each (macOS); `S18` under `M9`, 200 logs on macOS (`4a30ba4`), 30 in the report (`09977a4`) and 200 on Linux
  (run 2). It is a justification by the source plus a regression check, not a proof.
- Evidence outside the repository lives on one machine plus the GitHub artifact (90 days).

